# Cyclone Cache - fetched from GitHub main branch.
# Not pinned during development; pinned to a specific commit at release time.
# Pinned to the lease-based region-pinning commit:
# every disk-borrow read stamps a per-stripe lease (default T=5s, guaranteed
# floor 3T/4) and writers defer wraps while a lease is live, bounded by a 60s
# anti-starvation ceiling (counted in wraps_forced_past_lease).  Embedders
# holding a borrow longer than 3T/4 must call ReadHandle::renew_lease() at a
# cadence <= 3T/4 (wired via ReadResult::renew_lease() / ps_read_renew_lease()).
# Bumped: cache teardown now joins every background thread
# unconditionally — fixes a thread-leak/use-after-free and a teardown
# deadlock+race the previous bounded-join+leak masked (ASan/TSan-clean,
# per-component regression guards).
# Bumped: the directory insert/remove
# election now verifies the full stored key before electing update-in-place.
# Previously a (bucket, 12-bit tag, phase) collision between two distinct keys
# silently destroyed the victim entry's slot on insert — a deterministic,
# data-loss-class bug (reads were already key-verified, so the victim just
# vanished as a clean miss). Colliding keys now coexist; eviction only happens
# on a completely full bucket and is counted in the new
# tag_collision_evictions stat. Follow-ups add checked lease renewal
# (renew_read_lease_strict) and a Volume-lifetime UAF fix from the zero-copy
# GA work — additive API, existing renew_lease() wiring unchanged.
# Bumped: Volume::open() now makes
# non-creators wait for the creator to finish initialization — closes a
# multi-process cache-open race where a second opener could observe a
# half-initialized volume and corrupt it or spuriously fail initialization;
# the lock is dropped by the kernel on process death, so crash recovery is
# automatic. A follow-up removes the dead WriteAggregator path, reclaiming ~4 MB of
# committed RSS per cache stripe (no API impact; downstream consumers
# verified unreferenced).
# Bumped: borrow-scoped wrap gating — the
# per-stripe read lease now gates wraps only while read handles are actually
# outstanding (refcounted, released on handle close; ceiling-forced wraps
# reset leaked state), so cache writes no longer starve at capacity under
# steady reads. On-disk format unchanged (v1); adds the borrows_outstanding
# stats gauge (append-only C-struct extension). Pairs with the worker-side
# de-alias fix.
# Bumped: lock-free concurrent read path —
# the reader-side stripe lock and the directory global lock are replaced by
# per-bucket seqlocks and per-thread-sharded read counters + HitTracker, so
# read throughput scales with cores instead of collapsing under contention.
# Also drops the per-write fsync in multi-process mode and fixes auto
# stripe sizing (32MB granularity; derived stripe count persisted in the
# volume header and validated on open). Adds stripe_count/stripe_bytes
# stats (append-only C-struct extension). OPERATIONAL: the on-disk volume
# format major version is bumped, so existing cache volumes auto-reset
# (cold-wipe) on first open after upgrade AND on rollback — expect a
# one-time origin-fetch spike; prefer an off-peak deploy window.
# Bumped: heap-backs the CRC-validation cache so
# sizeof(Volume) drops from ~520 KB to ~8.5 KB — a stack-allocated Volume
# no longer overflows a 1 MB thread stack (the Windows default). One
# pointer indirection on the read path; no format or API change.
# Bumped: same fix for HitTracker — its 4096-stripe array
# made sizeof(HitTracker) ~1 MB under MSVC, so the stack-constructed
# trackers in cache_test silently died on Windows (1 MB default stack).
# Heap-backed; sizeof ~120 bytes; static_assert guard added.
# Bumped (zero-copy hardening batch): the first change adds the
# reader-side acquire fences the copy-then-verify epoch rechecks needed on
# weakly-ordered CPUs (x86 no-op) and makes forced-wrap deadline polling
# normative for aliased serving (the nginx barrier + worker de-alias
# changes already comply); the second closes a wrap-survivor window where a stale
# directory entry or chain node could hand out a borrow the ordinary
# forward fill then overwrote undetected (positional guard at both read
# choke points); the third stops the multi-process write lock from
# force-releasing a live-but-stalled holder (liveness proof +
# takeover-generation guard — overlapping-write fix).
# Bumped (wave-3 hardening batch): one change holds the
# per-stripe write lock across the pwrite, closing a microsecond
# reservation-to-pwrite tear window a concurrent reader could observe;
# Another routes in-place volume-header updates through the mapping on
# Windows, fixing fd writes to mmap'd ranges being silently swallowed on
# some Windows configurations (relevant to Windows/IIS embedders). The batch also
# add libFuzzer read-gauntlet + nightly stress harnesses and a Windows
# MSVC/AppVerifier CI lane upstream. On-disk format UNCHANGED: no cache
# reset on upgrade or rollback.
# Bumped to cyclone 330035e (the multi-process silent-corruption fix line
# plus the upgrade-safety stack): completes the fix line and makes
# cross-format upgrades unilaterally safe. One change fixes
# shared-cursor wrap adoption (a stale-high peer could re-wrap and reserve
# an OVERLAPPING range -> CRC-clean corruption); another adds the lifetime-lock
# reset gate (never reset a volume under a live peer -- refuse to open
# instead); the format bump moves the on-disk format to v6 (8-aligned header tail +
# document-slot padding) and fixes the header RMW races on the hit-count
# and remove-alternate paths; the stack encodes format+geometry into the cache
# FILENAME (cyclone.dat -> cyclone-6-<geohash>.dat) so binaries that
# disagree on layout open DIFFERENT files -- cross-format upgrade/restart
# overlap (incl. the nginx.so + factory_worker pair sharing one cache) can
# never corrupt a live peer's cache, regardless of release order; and adds
# opt-in startup GC of superseded fingerprint files
# (gc_superseded_on_start, default OFF -- not enabled here; a legacy
# cyclone.dat is never auto-deleted).
# OPERATIONAL: v6 is a format bump, but with fingerprinted filenames the
# new binary opens a NEW cache file rather than resetting the old one:
# cold cache on upgrade, previous file left orphaned on disk (reclaim
# manually or via the opt-in GC), and rollback is WARM (the old binary
# finds its own file untouched).
#
# Bumped: Cache::volume_files() exposes the actual
# (fingerprint-named) on-disk volume path — needed by lib/cache and the
# nginx module for chmod 0666, reset-by-delete, corrupt-cache recovery and
# the sendfile FD, all of which previously operated on the configured path
# and silently missed the fingerprinted file.  Also makes the size==0
# open-at-on-disk-size mode (how nginx opens the worker-created volume)
# resolve to the sized peers' fingerprinted file instead of the raw legacy
# path — without it, nginx and the worker silently stop sharing a volume.
#
# Bumped to cyclone edecf16. It lands the write when a
# directory bucket is completely full of current-phase entries: previously
# insert() returned false and the write was silently dropped, so
# small-object workloads could start losing writes on a volume only a few
# percent full by bytes; now the entry nearest the wrap cursor (the next
# to be overwritten anyway) is evicted so the write always lands. It also
# implements the cross-process reset gate on Windows via byte-range file
# locks (it was POSIX-only): an incompatible open can no longer reset a
# cache file out from under a live Windows peer — it refuses to open with
# the new tail-appended CacheError::ResetRefusedLivePeer instead of
# misreporting a version mismatch (MapError's default arm maps it to
# PS_ERR_INTERNAL; the open simply fails, drain the peer and retry) — and
# adds spawn-based multi-process tests for the gate. New observability:
# bucket_full_evictions, resets_under_degraded_gate, resets_gate_verified
# — ABI tail-appends to the stats structs, transparent to this repo's
# consumers (worker stats refresh and ps_cache_stats copy named fields
# only; the C# NativeCacheStats mirrors our own ps_cache_stats_t, not the
# cyclone struct). Surfacing the new counters in 2.0 stats is a separate
# follow-up. On-disk format UNCHANGED: no cache reset on upgrade or
# rollback.
#
# Bumped to cyclone 962f2e8 (24 commits). Four things matter here.
#
# 1. LICENCE. Cyclone is Apache-2.0 from the relicense commit onward; every
#    commit up to and including the previous pin was BUSL-1.1. The SBOM and
#    NOTICE in this repo describe the library as Apache-2.0, and at this pin
#    that is finally true of the commit we actually build.
#
# 2. BREAKING -- ON-DISK FORMAT MAJOR 7 (volume header and document in
#    lockstep). The volume filename carries the format major, so an upgraded
#    binary opens a NEW file rather than resetting the old one: every
#    deployment starts with an EMPTY CACHE that refills as traffic arrives.
#    Rollback stays warm (a format-6 binary reopens its own untouched file).
#    The superseded file is left on disk -- the opt-in startup GC of
#    superseded volumes is still default-off and not enabled here, so
#    reclaim it manually.
#
# 3. THE DEFECT THIS PIN FIXES, which has already bitten deployed caches.
#    Re-recording the same alternate id left every superseded document
#    linked in the chain forever: physical chain depth grew one per
#    re-record while the unique-id count stayed put, until the traversal cap
#    refused every further write to that key. The key then froze -- reads
#    kept serving the last-landed version with no error, so it was served
#    stale and never re-optimised, with no self-healing path. Post-wrap a
#    dominant key's chain could additionally turn cyclic and wedge earlier.
#    The write path now unlinks the superseded same-id node during the chain
#    walk it already performs (no extra I/O), refuses to link a new head to
#    nodes from a previous wrap epoch, and carries a single-id conditional
#    chain reset that lets an already-wedged chain accept writes again.
#    Splicing is best-effort: a splice that cannot proceed safely is
#    deferred and counted, never failing the caller's write. Default ON; the
#    upstream kill switch is not set here, and the wrap-frontier refusal and
#    the reset backstop stay active regardless.
#
# Also in the delta, reaching this repo with no config change on our side:
# This delta makes CacheConfig::max_object_size live (declared but never read
# until now), so the 64 MB default is ENFORCED and an over-size put fails
# with the tail-appended CacheError::ObjectTooLarge -- MapError's default
# arm maps it to PS_ERR_INTERNAL, so no code change is needed here. It also
# makes both built-in alternate selectors treat a non-empty
# acceptable_alternates set as a HARD restriction rather than a hint (this
# repo's lib/classify selector builds that set). It stops the RAM
# tier from serving an entry that a re-record or a removal should have
# invalidated. It zeroes a wrap deadline left uninitialised in
# MmapDirectory::init. And it adds opt-in cross-process RAM-cache coherence:
# default OFF and not plumbed through PageSpeedCacheConfig, so it is inert
# here -- exposing it is a separate follow-up. The lifetime fix plus the chain fix's four
# alternate-chain counters tail-append to the stats structs, transparent to
# this repo's field-by-field stats consumers; surfacing them is likewise a
# follow-up. CacheConfig::min_object_size was removed upstream; this
# repo never set it. The vendored build glue in //third_party:cyclone.BUILD
# needs no change: the delta adds test sources only, and the library's
# source set, header layout and include dirs are byte-identical.
#
# 4. A USE-AFTER-FREE ON THE WRITE PATH, fixed in the last commit of the delta.
#    The per-object bound now reads Volume::config() at the top of every
#    write, which turned the write handle's long-standing raw Volume* into a
#    live use-after-free the moment a handle outlived its volume -- a cache
#    reset or a teardown under an open handle, which the purge endpoint makes
#    operator-reachable. The handle now holds a weak reference, pins the volume
#    for the duration of write() and the commit, and reports
#    CacheError::Closed when the volume is gone. The sanitizer lane in this
#    repo reproduces the old fault and passes at this pin.
# Re-pointed to the public home: C1 is the orphan-landed sanitized tree;
# library content (src/, include/) identical to the source commit recorded
# on the orphan, src/include delta vs the previous pin comment-only.
new_git_repository(
    name = "cyclone",
    build_file = "//third_party:cyclone.BUILD",
    commit = "a1637f8c776556f8c2e1417aa52e3f635bbfe91d",
    remote = "https://github.com/We-Amp/cyclone-cache.git",
)

